{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\nOperators need an authoritative configuration precedence/source/lifecycle reference and a security reference, and the registry-derived parts of those docs must stay byte-current with the config schema and the ownership/secret-name registries rather than drifting by hand. `docs/config.md` is ALSO a hand-authored operator doc with many in-repo consumers: content-asserting tests (`tests/unit/test_snapshot_docs.py`, `test_session_id.py`, `test_structured_run.py`, `test_harness_provenance.py`, `test_session_shims.py`, `test_agents_claim_assignee_guidance.py`, `test_metrics_docs.py`, `test_tombstone_registry.py`, `test_build_drift.py`), anchor-fragment deep links (`AGENTS.md#ticketdefault_assignee`, `docs/reuse-surface.md#rebar_operation_snapshot_shadow`, `docs/concurrency.md#config-key-inventory`, `docs/event-schema.md`), and source-guidance references (`src/rebar/_config_coercion.py`, `_config_schema.py`, `_operation_config.py`, `llm/config.py`). A wholesale byte-generation of `config.md` would strand all of these.\n\n## Outcome\nA new `scripts/gen_config_reference.py` emits the registry-derived configuration reference into a single MARKER-DELIMITED generated region inside the existing `docs/config.md` (between `<!-- BEGIN GENERATED: config-reference -->` and `<!-- END GENERATED: config-reference -->`), leaving ALL existing hand-authored prose, section headings, and anchor slugs OUTSIDE the region byte-for-byte unchanged. It also generates a new indexed `docs/security.md` from the ownership and secret-name registries. Non-registry-derivable narrative topics named by the epic (observability exception, subprocess limitations, explicit behavior deltas, native refresh) are supplied VERBATIM from a curated in-repo template asset the generator embeds (not synthesized from the schema); registry-derived tables (precedence/source/lifecycle per config key, secret-name inventory) are computed from the schema + registries. `docs/README.md` and `docs/architecture.md` navigation reference the new/updated docs. A newline-normalizing drift check (portable, run under `make lint`, no CI dependency) fails when the generated region of `config.md` or `docs/security.md` is stale.\n\n## Plan\n1. Add `scripts/gen_config_reference.py` (modelled on `gen_env_registry.py`/`gen_cli_reference.py`): read the config schema + ownership + secret-name registries; render (a) the registry-derived tables and (b) a curated template asset (checked-in, holding the observability/subprocess/behavior-delta/native-refresh narrative verbatim) into the marker-delimited region of `docs/config.md` and into `docs/security.md`. The generator ONLY rewrites the delimited region of `config.md`; it never touches text outside the markers.\n2. Insert the marker pair into `docs/config.md` at a stable location that preserves every existing heading and anchor slug; create `docs/security.md`; update `docs/README.md` and `docs/architecture.md` navigation.\n3. Wire a drift check into `make lint` (portable/no-CI path, mirroring `check_docs_index.py`) that regenerates in-memory and compares to the checked-out file with newline normalization (universal-newline / text-mode read, not a raw byte compare) so a CRLF autocrlf checkout on Windows does not fail spuriously.\n\n## Testing (RED -> GREEN)\n- Drift: a deliberate edit inside the generated region trips the drift check; regenerating clears it. An edit OUTSIDE the markers does NOT trip it (proves hand-authored content is not owned by the generator).\n- Preservation: the existing content-asserting suites listed in Context still pass unchanged, and every enumerated anchor slug (`#ticketdefault_assignee`, `#rebar_operation_snapshot_shadow`, `#config-key-inventory`) still resolves to a heading in `config.md` after generation — asserted by a test that parses `config.md` headings and checks each required slug is present.\n- Topic coverage (concrete proving command): a test asserts the generated `config.md` region and `docs/security.md` each contain a heading/anchor for every named topic — an explicit REQUIRED_TOPICS list (config precedence, source, lifecycle, restart/redeploy rotation, native refresh, observability exception, subprocess limitations, behavior deltas, MCP/CLI exposure) checked by anchor-slug presence, failing if any is missing.\n- Newline safety: the drift check passes when the checked-out file is read with CRLF line endings (simulated) and the generator emits LF — a regression test feeds CRLF content and asserts no false drift.\n- Portability: the generator and drift check run under `make lint` with no CI provider present.\n\n## Compatibility / rollback\nDocs-and-tooling only; no product-behavior change. Existing `docs/config.md` hand-authored content, headings, and anchors are preserved (only a new delimited region is added). Rollback removes the generator, the curated template asset, `docs/security.md`, the marker region, and the drift-check wiring, restoring `docs/config.md` to its pre-change hand-authored form.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] `scripts/gen_config_reference.py` renders the registry-derived tables plus the curated template asset into a marker-delimited region of `docs/config.md` and into `docs/security.md`, and rewrites ONLY the delimited region of `config.md` (text outside the markers is byte-unchanged).\n- [ ] All existing `docs/config.md` consumers remain intact: the content-asserting suites named in Context pass unchanged, and every enumerated anchor slug still resolves to a heading (proven by a heading/anchor-presence test).\n- [ ] The generated `config.md` region and `docs/security.md` contain an anchor for every topic in the REQUIRED_TOPICS list (precedence, source, lifecycle, restart/redeploy rotation, native refresh, observability exception, subprocess limitations, behavior deltas, MCP/CLI exposure), proven by a topic-coverage test.\n- [ ] The drift check runs under `make lint` with no CI dependency, fails on a stale generated region, passes after regeneration, and does NOT false-fail on a CRLF checkout (newline-normalized comparison).\n- [ ] `docs/README.md` and `docs/architecture.md` navigation reference the new/updated docs."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787150195030942001,"uuid":"1e3f59b5-123d-449d-aefb-4d5f3a1ca01f"}